      },
      {
        "id": "p26-q7-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "At a party with $n$ guests, explain why there are $\\dfrac{n(n-1)}{2}$ hand shakes.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Each of the $n$ guests shakes hands with $n-1$ others. The product $n(n-1)$ counts every hand shake twice, so divide by 2.",
          "worked": "For each of the $n$ guests there are $n-1$ other guests, giving $n(n-1)$ counts. Each hand shake is counted once for each of its two participants. Therefore the number of hand shakes is $\\dfrac{n(n-1)}{2}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p26-q7-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "At Bill’s party there were 190 hand shakes. How many people were at the party?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "20 people.\n\nUse the handshake formula and solve the resulting quadratic, keeping the positive solution.",
          "worked": "Let $n$ be the number of people.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}&=190\\\\n(n-1)&=380\\\\n^2-n-380&=0\\\\(n-20)(n+19)&=0\\\\n&=20\\text{ or }n=-19\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe number of people is positive, so there were 20 people.\n\nCheck: $\\dfrac{20\\times19}{2}=190$ hand shakes.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 66
      }
    ]
